[{:rule-id "rule-ai", :item {:feed-id "hn", :item-id "https://www.anthropic.com/research/formal-verification", :title "LLMs are making formal software verification feasible at a much larger scale", :link "https://www.anthropic.com/research/formal-verification", :published-at #inst "2026-10-22T22:54:43.000-00:00", :content "Comments"}, :excerpts [{:text "LLMs are making formal software verification feasible...", :matched-terms ["llm"], :source :title}]} {:rule-id "rule-ai", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://kirahowe.com/2026/oct/22/editmd-0-1-0", :title "editmd 0.1.0", :link "https://kirahowe.com/2026/oct/22/editmd-0-1-0", :published-at #inst "2026-10-23T02:04:19.000-00:00", :content "I made a markdown editor for MacOS! With Claude writing most of my code now I spend a lot more time than I used to editing docs. I prefer working in markdown, and wanted a really simple wysiwyg editor that works like a word processor but saves files as markdown. I often work with agents on docs now, so I needed an editor that would not break my workflow if the file was edited while open. I will often be writing and have an agent go search for references, for example, or add links and references, or fill in quotes that I only vaguely remember, or add code examples to a spec, or any number of other things, so I wanted something that would let me keep working seamlessly while the file I had open was also being editing by agents at the same time. Most editors either clobber one side or throw up a \"the file changed on disk\" dialog. editmd uses Y.js to merge your agents edits in real time, like you&aposre collaborating on a google doc. If an agent edits the file while it&aposs open, its changes just appear, your unsaved edits stay put, and Cmd-Z only undoes your own changes, never the agent&aposs. Try it out It needs macOS 14 or later, and runs on both Apple silicon and Intel: brew install --cask kirahowe/tap/editmd\nopen -a editmd notes.md\n It&aposs not signed with an Apple Developer ID (yet?), so the cask clears the quarantine flag for you. If you grab the zip from the release page directly, run xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine editmd.app before opening it. There&aposs also a little website at kirahowe.github.io/editmd with some elaboration of the features. How it works It&aposs a native AppKit document app with a WebKit view running a Milkdown editor (ProseMirror + remark), all bundled into the app so nothing loads from the network. The document in each window is a Y.js CRDT. When the file changes on disk, editmd diffs the new text against the version it last saw, replays the difference as edits block by block, and lets Y.js merge them with whatever you&aposve typed since. Edits to different paragraphs, list items or table rows combine cleanly. Documents autosave one second after you stop typing (or every five seconds if you don&apost stop). A file you open and save without editing keeps its exact bytes. An edited one keeps its encoding (UTF-8, UTF-16 or Windows-1252), its line endings, its YAML or TOML front matter, reference-style links and tables. GitHub flavoured markdown works so tables, task lists, strikethrough, footnotes, and GitHub&aposs heading anchors all render properly, so a table of contents written for GitHub works as expected. It also supports mermaid diagrams so fenced mermaid blocks render as diagrams with a live preview while you edit them. Clicking on a diagram shows the raw markdown, or pressing Cmd-/ flips the entire doc to the raw markdown. It&aposs also safe to open external (i.e. untrusted) documents. Cmd+click on a link never launches anything -- text files open in editmd, and any other file, folder, or app is just shown in Finder. Remote images are blocked until you allow them, like in Mail, so a document can&apost leak any information via an image fetch. On using AI to ship software I have found working with AI to build software increasingly fun. Obviously this was built with heavy assistance from Claude (I primarily use Opus 5.5 right now), but it is still very tailored to my taste and needs. I am also coming to appreciate how much better at coding Claude is than I ever was. It&aposs kind of sad to admit defeat, but it&aposs really just true that it catches things I don&apost now. Once I thought this was ready for release, I asked Claude to QA rigorously before putting up a release, and it found a bunch of stuff I definitely would have shipped: If WebKit&aposs content process crashed, the window reloaded stale text, and the next autosave silently overwrote changes that had already been saved Cmd-clicking a link to an .app in a cloned repo would launch it, with no Gatekeeper prompt since cloned files aren&apost quarantined Opening a document fetched its remote images, i.e. tracking pixels Windows-1252 files got silently converted to UTF-8, and UTF-16 files opened as garbage Running 485 generated markdown cases through the serializer turned up real content loss: badges ([![build](…)](…)) lost their links, tables with ragged rows shifted their columns, and unused link definitions just disappeared Normally these are the kinds of issues I would have never noticed until they screwed over a real user, but we don&apost have to accept these kinds of problems in our software anymore! I just told Claude to spin up a bunch of parallel agents to fix all these problems and it fixed seven different issues, each in its own git worktree, each one going through implementation → independent code review + adversarial QA → fix, until two reviewer agents signed off. It ran 30 sub agents and took about two and a half hours. I had a main agent doing integration, which still caught yet another real bug -- two of these workflows had each taken over the markdown serializer, and one of them had broken reference links. Every fix came with regression tests, and the test suite grew from 65 web tests to 762. Known limitations There are still some known issues due to the fundamental complexities of merging concurrent edits to a single document: If you and another program edit the same paragraph at the same moment, the paragraph keeps both changes, which may need manual tidying. A save checks the file one last time before writing, but an outside write that lands in the few microseconds between that check and the write still gets overwritten. Nothing can realistically close that gap without the other program&aposs cooperation. Links with custom schemes (obsidian://, file://) don&apost do anything on Cmd-click in the rendered view yet. Very large files (hundreds of KB) are fine to type in, but the first autosave after opening still serializes the whole document. This is the very first release, so there are definitely still rough edges, but I&aposm already using this every day now. If you try it and run into anything, please open an issue on the repo. Thanks for reading! release (link) / #coding-agents #developer-tools #editmd #macos #markdown #open-source #release"}, :excerpts [{:text "I made a markdown editor for MacOS! With Claude writing most of my code now I spend a lot more...", :matched-terms ["claude"], :source :content} {:text "... this was built with heavy assistance from Claude (I primarily use Opus 5.5 right now), but it is...", :matched-terms ["claude"], :source :content} {:text "... coming to appreciate how much better at coding Claude is than I ever was. It&aposs kind of sad to admit...", :matched-terms ["claude"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "clojure", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/vimslime", :title "vim-slime", :link "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/vimslime", :published-at #inst "2026-10-23T03:43:13.000-00:00", :content "<!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>Tarn Barford</title> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"/> <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/x-icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\"> <link href=\"/style.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"application/atom+xml\" title=\"Journals of Tarn Barford\" href=\"/atom\" /> <link href=\"/highlight.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link href=\"/highlight-console.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> </head> <body> <div id=\"container\"> <div id=\"header\"> <div id=\"header\"> <p>From the <a href=\"/journal\">Journals</a> of <a href=\"/\">Tarn Barford</a></p> <h1> vim-slime </h1> <p> Mar 26, 2012 </p> </div> </div> <div id=\"post_content\"> <html><body><p>Today I found the awesomeness that is <a href=\"https://github.com/jpalardy/vim-slime\">vim-slime</a>, it's been an exciting day for me. <a href=\"http://common-lisp.net/project/slime/\">Slime</a> is the \"The Superior Lisp Interaction Mode for Emacs\", I can almost hear the emacs crowd laughing.</p> <p>For those that use vim and haven't used Slime, vim-slime or <a href=\"https://github.com/vim-scripts/VimClojure\">something similar</a>, this is why it's awesome:</p> <p><strong>Text can be sent from any process to the stdin of a <a href=\"http://www.gnu.org/software/screen/\">gnu screen</a> or <a href=\"http://tmux.sourceforge.net/\">tmux</a> session. The process in this case is vim and the screen/tmux session is a terminal</strong>.</p> <p>Screen is a <a href=\"/journal/oh-screen-where-have-you-been\">really neat</a> terminal multiplexer (you can run multiple terminals in a terminal window). The multiplexed shell processes are children of the screen process, which itself is not a child of the terminal window process. This means a screen process and its child processes keep running if you close the terminal window. Later you can re-connect to it, this is what makes vim-slime possible.</p> <p>Here is an screen shot, on the left is me in gVim writing some awful Clojure <a href=\"#footnote-1\">[1]</a>. On the right is a screen buffer in which I started a Clojure REPL. When I want to try run some code I can send any vim text selection to the REPL in a keystroke (or two).</p> <p><img alt=\"vim slime screenshot\" src=\"screenshot.jpg\"/></p> <p>It doesn't have to be a Clojure REPL either, we can send anything to a screen shell. We could run git commands, find, grep, sed, etc. Like with the Clojure REPL we can even interact with any terminal programs that use STDIN.</p> <p>This concept can be taken even further, You can even connect to a tmux session over SSH and share a terminal or a <a href=\"http://remotepairprogramming.com/remote-pair-programming-with-tmux-and-vim-the\">terminal program like vim to do remote pairing</a>!</p> <p>Hopefully remote pairing is the topic of my next post as there are a couple geographically distant people I know who are keen to do some pair hacking. I stand to learn a lot!</p> <p><a name=\"footnote-1\">[1]</a> I learnt almost everything I know about Lisp from <a href=\"http://www.ccs.neu.edu/home/matthias/BTLS/\">The Little Schemer</a>. Great book.</p></body></html> </div> <div id=\"comments\"> </div> </div> <div id=\"footer\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Questions, comments, suggestions? Email me, <a href=\"mailto:tarn@tarnbarford.net\">tarn@tarnbarford.net</a> (<a href=\"/pgp.txt\">public key</a>)</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </div> </body> </html>"}, :excerpts [{:text "... or <a href=\"https://github.com/vim-scripts/VimClojure\">something similar</a>, this is why it's...", :matched-terms ["clojure"], :source :content} {:text "... on the left is me in gVim writing some awful Clojure <a href=\"#footnote-1\">[1]</a>. On the right is a screen buffer in which I started a Clojure REPL. When I want to try run some code I can send any vim text selection to the REPL in a keystroke (or two).</p> <p><img alt=\"vim...", :matched-terms ["clojure" "repl"], :source :content} {:text "... <p>It doesn't have to be a Clojure REPL either, we can send anything to a screen shell. We could run git commands, find, grep, sed, etc. Like with the Clojure REPL we can even interact with any terminal programs...", :matched-terms ["clojure" "repl" "command"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "rescript", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/swipe", :title "Swipe Keyboard", :link "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/swipe", :published-at #inst "2026-10-23T03:43:13.000-00:00", :content "<!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>Tarn Barford</title> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"/> <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/x-icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\"> <link href=\"/style.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"application/atom+xml\" title=\"Journals of Tarn Barford\" href=\"/atom\" /> <link href=\"/highlight.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link href=\"/highlight-console.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <style> #swipe-canvas { position: relative; width: 900px; height: 300px; } #swipe-results { font-size: 30px; padding-left: 50px; padding-left: 50px; } #swipe-results ul { margin: 0px; padding: 0px; } #swipe-results li { float: left; background-color: #DDDDDD; list-style-type: none; padding: 10px; margin: 5px; border-radius: 5px; } #swipe { position: relative; } #swipe-loading { position: absolute; height: 50px; width: 300px; top: 85px; left: 300px; background-color: darkgray; border-radius: 10px; text-align: center; padding-top: 20px; border: black; border-width: 5px; } </style> </head> <body> <div id=\"container\"> <div id=\"header\"> <div id=\"header\"> <p>From the <a href=\"/journal\">Journals</a> of <a href=\"/\">Tarn Barford</a></p> <h1> Swipe Keyboard </h1> <p> Apr 06, 2014 </p> </div> </div> <div id=\"post_content\"> <html><body><p>When I first tried a <a href=\"http://www.swype.com/\">Swype</a> keyboard I was impressed how effective it was. Even though I don't use the feature on my phone I was interested in how it could be built, so I <a href=\"https://github.com/tarnacious/swipe-keyboard\">implemented this otherwise useless swipe-able keyboard</a> below. It probably doesn't work on mobile devices, but works on modern browsers with mouse pointers (although I've only really tried Chrome and Firefox).</p> <div id=\"swipe\"> <canvas height=\"300px\" id=\"swipe-canvas\" width=\"900px\"></canvas> <div id=\"swipe-results\"></div> <div style=\"clear: both\"></div> <h2 id=\"swipe-loading\">Loading<noscript>Javascript is Required</noscript></h2> </div> <p>I initially tried to solve this using the technique Peter Norvig famously uses in his <a href=\"http://norvig.com/spell-correct.html]\">spell checker</a>. He takes a sequence of characters and generates a set of word candidates by adding, removing and swapping characters in the original sequence, the generated candidates are removed if they are not found a dictionary. This can work but to be effective too many combinations need to be generated.</p> <p>If the dictionary is indexed into a <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trie\">trie</a> the number of combinations generated can be reduced significantly by traversing the trie and only generating valid letter combinations. This is a pretty bare implementation of that, it requires: </p> <ul> <li>The first and last characters of the initial sequence are used </li> <li>Intermediate characters in the initial sequence can be repeated or discarded </li> <li>No characters are added or swapped</li> </ul> <p>Basically, if you swipe through all the characters in a word in order, then the word will be found if it is in the index regardless how many characters are swiped in between. It is surprisingly quick and effective.</p> <p>This implementation uses <a href=\"https://raw.github.com/first20hours/google-10000-english/master/google-10000-english.txt\">these 10000 words</a>, I intended to use digital books but never got around to it as these words demonstrate the concept well enough.</p> <p>This is the first thing I've written in <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">ClojureScript</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">Clojure</a>, so my code my vary from non-idiomatic to shamblolic. I initially used a <a href=\"http://clojuredocs.org/clojure_core/clojure.zip/zipper\">zipper</a> to build the trie with immutable data structures, but found the indexing took to long with my zipper implementation so I <a href=\"https://github.com/tarnacious/swipe-keyboard/commit/6edd7b26e78121fbe8586b3f0ef54ca8277d9e32\">switched to using native Javascript maps</a>.</p> <p>I found that <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/core.async\">core.async</a> library is really awesome, the <a href=\"http://docs.closure-library.googlecode.com/git/index.html\">Google closure library</a> and <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/closure/compiler/\">compiler</a> integration with <a href=\"http://leiningen.org/\">Leiningen</a> the <a href=\"https://github.com/emezeske/lein-cljsbuild\">cljsbuild plug-in</a> to be impressive. My main pains were the slow JVM start-up time, the advanced closure compiler build of the web worker script fails silently when run (but the main script works fine when compiled with the advanced compiler), and at times I felt some compile time type checking would be nice.</p> <p>I would like to extend this experiment to index the word occurrence counts and proceeding word counts in original text and rank the found words as most likely. Support casing, umlauts, special characters, spelling correction and compound words in the indexing and lookup. I think a live lookup while swiping would also be possible.</p> <p>Overall this was fun, turned out OK I think, and was a great learning experience.</p></body></html> </div> <div id=\"comments\"> </div> </div> <div id=\"footer\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Questions, comments, suggestions? Email me, <a href=\"mailto:tarn@tarnbarford.net\">tarn@tarnbarford.net</a> (<a href=\"/pgp.txt\">public key</a>)</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </div> <script src=\"swipe.js\" type=\"text/javascript\"></script> </body> </html>"}, :excerpts [{:text "... <h2 id=\"swipe-loading\">Loading<noscript>Javascript is Required</noscript></h2> </div> <p>I initially...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content} {:text "... in <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">ClojureScript</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">Clojure</a>, so my code my vary from...", :matched-terms ["rescript"], :source :content} {:text "... to using native Javascript maps</a>.</p> <p>I found that <a...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content}]}]